  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Acknowledging the Constraints**\n\nI\'ve just received clear instructions: Google Stitch screens are the definitive visual source. No redesign, new language, or simplification allowed! I\'m now laser-focused on fully grasping and respecting these boundaries. I need to be sure I understand the \"why\" behind the constraints. This is essential for a compliant approach.\n\n\n**Defining the Scope**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the precise boundaries of the project. It\'s frontend only, preserving the existing Stitch design as a cohesive React web application. The core function is Photo upload to a custom brick moc, generating LDR, PDF instructions, and parts list. I understand that conversational AI, prompt editing, or model selection are out of scope. Unsupported claims in the mockups are also being replaced. I\'m focusing on responsiveness for desktop (1440px) and mobile (375px).\n\n\n**Analyzing the Specs**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the provided Stitch screens, specifically focusing on the result screen and its elements, meticulously mapping each component for frontend implementation. I\'m verifying that the design includes the interactive 3D CAD stage within a 12-column grid, taking detailed notes on the user interface, paying special attention to the responsiveness requirements for both desktop and mobile. I will translate this information in Javascript and React.\n\n\n**Detailing the Components**\n\nI\'m now cataloging all the components of the result screen\'s right-hand panel, including the \"MODEL SUMMARY\" card with its key metrics: part count, unique types, build steps, and color breakdown. I will also incorporate the \"Buildability\" verification, and digital deliverables, with download links for the PDF, LDraw, and parts list.\n\n\n**Refining the Preview**\n\nI\'m now integrating the mobile \"Order Preview\" screen (Screen 2), focusing on the step indicator, image preview, and order specifications. I\'ve replaced the fake ordering claim. I\'m also ensuring the correct inclusion of all digital deliverables, including the 3D model, PDF booklet, and parts list, as well as a neutral guarantee. The \"Build My Model\" CTA and relevant badges are being implemented.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Workflow**\n\nI\'m now outlining the \"Assembly Rig\" and \"Transformation sandbox\" sections, focusing on the visual storytelling of the model creation process. I\'m prioritizing the progress bar\'s dynamic display for \"Validating Structural Load & Part Availability\" at 72%. I\'m currently working on the \"Build Assembly Pipeline\" steps.\n\n\n**Structuring the Flow**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Photo & Order Preview\" screen using a segmented indicator. I\'m focusing on the \"Review\" stage with the photo and its attributes, including an optical grid overlay. I\'m noting the quality assessment with clear, concise descriptions. The \"Ready to Build\" card contains the shape verification, complexity, physical scale, and the components, including the 3D model, step-by-step PDF, and digital BOM parts list. I\'m confirming the pricing and implementing the call-to-action button, along with the neutral, reassuring copy.\n\n\n**Designing the Landing**\n\nI\'m now detailing the landing page, starting with the header, nav, and upload button. The hero section will feature the \"Instant Physical Brick Generator\" badge. I\'m focusing on \"Your Photo. Built in Bricks.\" message and description, along with the visual transformation stage. This includes a memory photo, an arrow badge, and a precision brick model. Finally, I\'m building out the upload action bar.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 6
      nanos: 712140267
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
