  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Visual Requirements**\n\nOkay, Tailwind v4 compiles, that\'s a good start! Now I\'m carefully reviewing the requirements, specifically focusing on the \"Approved visual source of truth\" guidelines. It seems I need to avoid any redesign, invention, simplification, or alteration of the existing typography, colors, and spacing. This is a very targeted visual constraint.\n\n\n**Defining Responsive Structure**\n\nI\'ve been mapping out the product flow and responsive targets. It looks like the supplied Stitch HTML and image references are the key source. Desktop is 1440px and mobile is 375px. Where Stitch only provided one view, I\'ll infer responsive behavior from the existing design system to keep things coherent. I have both viewports for the Order Preview, which is a great starting point for that flow!\n\n\n**Refining Technical Scope**\n\nThe Stitch markup is proving surprisingly helpful, especially the responsive classes. It looks like the team did a great job defining the basic structure. I\'m now digging into the core product flow, specifically the photo-to-brick-mockup pipeline, and clarifying the scope. We\'re keeping things simple: no AI-driven features like conversational interfaces or prompt editors. I\'m also carefully crafting text replacements for any unsupported claims, such as guarantees or fake statistics, ensuring accuracy and avoiding overpromising. I am using placeholders when necessary.\n\n\n**Defining Frontend Architecture**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the frontend architecture and data structures. I\'ve defined the `src/types.ts` file, establishing the `Screen` type for application flow. I\'ve also laid out the data model types, covering things like model metadata, pipeline steps, and download triggers. My plan is to include a mock data file, specifically the \"Vintage Instant Camera\" example, which will support user upload and example photo selection. I\'m focusing on the `Header.tsx` component, the initial entry-point.\n\n\n**Defining Component Scope**\n\nI\'ve been working on the component structure, creating new files as needed. Specifically, I\'ve defined `Footer.tsx` with the shared BrickForge footer from the Stitch HTML. The `LandingView.tsx` component is now implemented, reflecting the approved Landing page structure. I\'m also deep into `UploadModal.tsx`, the brick-styled upload dialog, complete with drag-and-drop support. I\'m also working on both `PreviewView.tsx` and `GeneratingView.tsx`.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 3
      nanos: 893266856
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
